Layer 2 — Data Link
Purpose

Handles communication across a local link/network.

Examples:

Ethernet
Wi-Fi
VLAN

It uses MAC addresses.

Example:

Source MAC:
AA:BB:CC:11:22:33

Destination MAC:
AA:BB:CC:44:55:66
Frame

The Data Link layer takes the Layer 3 packet:

IP Header + TCP Header + Data

and places it inside a Layer 2 frame.

Conceptually:

┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────┬──────────┐
│ Ethernet     │ IP Packet                   │ Trailer  │
│ Header       │                              │          │
└──────────────┴──────────────────────────────┴──────────┘

The result is called a frame.

Switch

A switch primarily works at Layer 2.

It learns MAC addresses:

MAC Address            Port

AA:AA:AA:AA:AA:AA  →   Port 1
BB:BB:BB:BB:BB:BB  →   Port 2

When a frame arrives, the switch checks the destination MAC and forwards it toward the appropriate port.

VLAN

VLAN is a Layer 2 concept.

Example:

VLAN 10 → Developers
VLAN 20 → Testing
VLAN 30 → Production

A VLAN creates a separate Layer 2 broadcast domain.

ARP

For IPv4, ARP maps an IP address to a MAC address on the local network.

Example:

Who has 192.168.1.1?
        ↓
Router:
192.168.1.1
MAC = AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF

Then the computer can create the local Ethernet/Wi-Fi frame.

ARP is commonly associated with Layer 2 because it supports local IP-to-MAC resolution, though it does not fit perfectly into the seven-layer model.

